Randomness in JavaScript — Why Math.random() Isn't Random Enough


Building a fair lottery picker requires more than Math.random(). Here's the difference between pseudo-random and cryptographically secure random, and when it matters.
Math.random(); // 0.7234...

This uses a PRNG (pseudo-random number generator) — typically xorshift128+ in V8. It's fast but deterministic: given the same seed, the sequence is reproducible.
For games and UI animations, this is fine. For lottery picks and security tokens, it isn't.
function secureRandom() {
  const array = new Uint32Array(1);
  crypto.getRandomValues(array);
  return array[0] / (0xFFFFFFFF + 1); // 0 to 1
}

This uses the OS entropy pool (hardware events, timing jitter) — genuinely unpredictable.
The naive shuffle (sort(() => Math.random() - 0.5)) is biased. Use Fisher-Yates:
function fairShuffle(array) {
  const arr = [...array];
  for (let i = arr.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(secureRandom() * (i + 1));
    [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
  }
  return arr;
}

function drawWithoutReplacement(items, count) {
  if (count > items.length) throw new Error('Cannot draw more than available');
  return fairShuffle(items).slice(0, count);
}

function drawWithReplacement(items, count) {
  return Array.from({ length: count }, () => {
    const index = Math.floor(secureRandom() * items.length);
    return items[index];
  });
}

Simple modulo creates bias because the range rarely divides evenly into the max integer:
function secureRandomInt(min, max) {
  const range = max - min + 1;
  const maxValid = Math.floor(0x100000000 / range) * range;
  let value;
  do {
    const arr = new Uint32Array(1);
    crypto.getRandomValues(arr);
    value = arr[0];
  } while (value >= maxValid); // Reject values in the biased range
  return min + (value % range);
}

